WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
Climbing frames like this,

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.504
Americans call them jungle gyms,

00:00:03.504 --> 00:00:04.918
they used to be all over,

00:00:04.918 --> 00:00:07.080
in kids' playgrounds and schools around the world.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.480
These days, they've mostly been replaced

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.020
by designs that are a little less geometric
and perhaps a little more safe.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.540
But this: this is the first one.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:16.920
Someone had to invent it.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.040
And that someone was called Sebastian Hinton.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.500
Technically, there was at least
one prototype before this one,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.280
but this was the first solidly-constructed,
modern climbing frame,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.160
the type that was sold under the
trademarked name of "Jungle Gym".

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.760
It was installed at a school here
in Winnetka, near Chicago, in 1920,

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.540
was used for 90 years, including being
relocated to another school,

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.020
and it now sits here, in the
Winnetka Historical Society's back garden.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.640
I'm not allowed to climb up this,
by the way, partly because it's historic,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.120
and partly because I really don't think
my US medical insurance

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.600
would cover someone in his thirties
falling off a jungle gym.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.380
Now, this was a pretty good
invention for the time.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:00.480
As the patent says, "the facilities for climbing
available to the average child are limited,

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.700
"and generally speaking somewhat dangerous".

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.320
"I have reduced--"

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.420
I don't know why I'm doing that voice!

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.520
He was American in the early 20th century,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.420
he wouldn't have sounded like...

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.560
..."I have reduced the danger of climbing
to a minimum."

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.160
For 1920, he was probably right,
but he is optimistic.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.220
The patent mentions that you might
tie some ropes across the bottom

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.420
to help catch a kid if they fall,

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.160
or put fence wire round the outside,

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.320
or even put it around
a swimming pool, but, ach,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.400
that's probably not needed.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.080
"A child falling in the structure

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.900
"will have so many opportunities
to catch a bar or vertical

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.080
"that it is almost impossible that
he will fall through it to the ground."

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.900
The idea of a child slipping,
hitting their head,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.560
and then ragdolling down to the floor
doesn't seem to have occurred to him.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.100
But he does emphasise safety,
and that it must be constructed

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.440
with the strength to handle
dozens of children

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.760
swinging around on it together.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.540
He covered his bases. The text
of the patent expands to cover

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.540
basically any arrangement
of perpendicular pipes.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.460
Like -- ow -- these.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:00.000
There's also a reference in there
to playing "three dimensional tag",

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.160
and that's where the story
gets interesting for me.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.560
Because while Sebastian Hinton
patented this,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.400
he got the idea from his father,
mathematician Charles Hinton.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.860
Charles has an interesting biography.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.380
He was fascinated with the idea
of the fourth dimension.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.420
He wrote the first popular essay
on it in 1880,

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.300
he invented the word
'tesseract' for a 4D cube,

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.980
along with the words
'ana' and 'kata',

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.420
as the 4D equivalent
of 'left' and 'right'.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.400
That was all Charles Hinton.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.920
Who also invented the first
baseball pitching machine...

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.460
...although it was powered by gunpowder
and reports differ

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.980
on how well it worked
and how many injuries it caused.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.320
Charles was also convicted of bigamy

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.240
for being married to two women
at the same time.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.120
Like I said, interesting biography.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.400
Anyway, according to Sebastian,
when he was growing up,

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.820
his father Charles built what was
basically a jungle gym out of bamboo,

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.560
a big structure of cubes like this.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.540
And Charles would get the children
to play a game:

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:53.040
he'd call out mathematical co-ordinates
on the grid, something like "X3 Y2 Z4!",

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.440
and the kids would have to race
to the first to get there.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.500
His logic was that humans mostly
walk around in two dimensions,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.540
but we can understand three.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.780
So perhaps if you can get kids to
move around three dimensions intuitively,

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.040
while their brains are developing,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.740
they'd be able to understand four.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.220
Did it work? Well, not in that way,
the kids were much more interested

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.440
with just playing around with it
as a climbing frame,

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.260
and none of them apparently developed
any four-dimensional insight.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.740
But years later, the adult
Sebastian patented this.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.440
It's one of those inventions that
had to happen at some point:

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.540
if not here, someone else would
have figured it out.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.200
But the size, shape, and design that
was in schoolyards around the world?

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.840
That's down to Sebastian Hinton, his dad,
and an attempt to teach the fourth dimension.

